{"name":"Coyote Wild","hex":"#ae9671","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"coyote","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Canis latrans, a species of canine native to North America.","A smuggler of undocumented immigrants across the land border from Latin America into the United States of America."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prospect for gold by manually digging holes into overlying earth, as into a hillside."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/coyote"},{"term":"wild","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Untamed; not domesticated.","Especially, being of the wild type: being of an unbroken ancestral line of undomesticated animals, as opposed to being feral, being an undomesticated animal whose ancestors were domesticated."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Inaccurately; not on target.","Intended to be synchronized with film or video but recorded separately."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wild"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coyote","blocks":[{"text":"The coyote is a species of canine also known as the American jackal, prairie wolf, and brush wolf. It is native to North America, and it is smaller than its close relative, the gray wolf, and slightly smaller than the closely related eastern wolf and red wolf. It fills much of the same ecological niche as the golden jackal does in Eurasia, but the coyote is generally larger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg/330px-2009-Coyote-Yosemite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coyote"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif/500px-Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Coyote_expansion_from_1900_to_2016.gif/1280px-